\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A nilpotent translation difference in characteristic p}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let #{k=\mathbb F_p}, let the uniform period be
#{N=p}, and set #{\tau=\tau_{\varepsilon_1}}.
Then #{\tau^p=\bbI}. The characteristic-#{p}
binomial identity gives
##{(\tau-\bbI)^p=0.}
The difference #{\tau-\bbI} is nonzero because
it sends #{\delta_0} to
#{\delta_{-\varepsilon_1}-\delta_0}, with distinct
supports. Thus the commutative translation algebra
has a nonzero nilpotent ideal generated by this
difference.}

\p{For completeness, if its regular module were
semisimple, that ideal would be a direct summand.
The corresponding module projection #{\pr} is
multiplication by #{\pr(1)}, and idempotence gives
#{\pr(1)^2=\pr(1)}. Its image is the ideal,
so #{\pr(1)} lies in the nilpotent ideal and hence
#{\pr(1)^p=0}. Idempotence then forces #{\pr(1)=0},
contradicting the nonzero ideal. The faithful
generated-algebra calculation in \ref{fdip-00W3}
therefore does not require semisimplicity.}
